# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = '2 Corinthians'
TESTAMENT = 'New Testament'
GENRE = 'letters'
SUBTITLE = 'Comfort, the Ministry of Reconciliation, and Strength in Weakness'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', '2 Corinthians is a letter from “Paul an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God, and Timothy our brother” to “the church of God which is at Corinth, with all the saints which are in all Achaia” (2 Cor. 1:1). Paul writes about his affliction and comfort, his change of plans, the ministry he has received, the collection for the saints, and the defense of his apostleship against those who questioned it.'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', '2 Corinthians is Paul’s letter to a Gentile church during the Acts 9–28 overlap. His ministry belongs to the Mystery Program, and the letter is read with the standing boundary on the new covenant: the covenant belongs to the house of Israel and the house of Judah (Jer. 31:31–34), and the Body benefits from Christ’s blood without being the covenant party.')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of 2 Corinthians', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the letter — how Paul begins, how his instructions develop, and how the letter builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Comfort in Affliction and the Ministry (Chs. 1–7)'), ('p', 'Paul blesses the God of all comfort, explains his delay, describes the ministry of the new testament and the glory of Christ, and speaks of the ministry of reconciliation (2 Cor. 1:3–4; 3:6; 4:6–7; 5:17–21).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Collection for the Saints (Chs. 8–9)'), ('p', 'Paul sets the Macedonians’ giving beside Christ’s grace and gives directions for a willing gift (2 Cor. 8:1–9; 9:6–8).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — The Defense of His Apostleship (Chs. 10–13)'), ('p', 'Paul answers his critics, recounts his labours, a vision, and a thorn in the flesh, and calls on the Corinthians to examine themselves (2 Cor. 11:2, 23–28; 12:1–10; 13:5).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in 2 Corinthians', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas Paul emphasizes in this book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how the Body of Christ should think and live. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas Paul wants believers to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The God of All Comfort</b> — God comforts us in all our tribulation so that we may comfort others (2 Cor. 1:3–4).', '<b>The Ministry of the New Testament</b> — Paul and his companions are “able ministers of the new testament” (2 Cor. 3:6).', '<b>Treasure in Earthen Vessels</b> — The light of the knowledge of God’s glory is held in earthen vessels (2 Cor. 4:6–7).', '<b>A New Creature</b> — “If any man be in Christ, he is a new creature” (2 Cor. 5:17).', '<b>Strength in Weakness</b> — “My grace is sufficient for thee: for my strength is made perfect in weakness” (2 Cor. 12:9).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in 2 Corinthians', [('p', 'This section gathers what the letter teaches about Christ — who He is, what He has done, and how Paul presents Him to the church. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered focus before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these verses together, you get a clear picture of how 2 Corinthians lifts up Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Image of God</b> — Christ “is the image of God” (2 Cor. 4:4).', '<b>The One Who Died for All</b> — One died for all, and those who live should live unto Him who died for them (2 Cor. 5:14–15).', '<b>Made Sin for Us</b> — God made Him “to be sin for us, who knew no sin; that we might be made the righteousness of God in him” (2 Cor. 5:21).', '<b>Rich, Yet Poor</b> — Though He was rich, for our sakes He became poor (2 Cor. 8:9).', '<b>The Judgment Seat</b> — We must all appear before the judgment seat of Christ (2 Cor. 5:10).'])]),
(5, 'The Church, the Law, and Israel in 2 Corinthians', [('p', 'This section helps you see how 2 Corinthians speaks about the church, the law, and Israel without mixing their programs. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read 2 Corinthians in its own setting — as a Body-of-Christ letter — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about Israel’s program.'), ('p', 'The letter uses Israel’s own history and the word “new testament” without placing the Body inside Israel’s covenants:'), ('ul', ['Paul and his companions are “ministers of the new testament” (2 Cor. 3:6). The covenant itself belongs to the house of Israel and the house of Judah (Jer. 31:31–34); Paul’s ministry stays with the Mystery.', 'The Body benefits from Christ’s blood without being a party to Israel’s New Covenant.', 'The reading of Moses and the veil is set beside the turning to the Lord (2 Cor. 3:12–18). The study reports it as written and assigns no program beyond the passage’s own audience.', 'The letter does not make the Body a party to any Israelite covenant.']), ('p', 'This reading rests on the passage’s own audience markers and setting.')]),
(6, 'Body-of-Christ Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in 2 Corinthians that belong uniquely to the Body of Christ — truths Paul gives to the church in this present age. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how 2 Corinthians contributes to the doctrine and order of the Body of Christ without borrowing from Israel’s prophetic program.'), ('p', '2 Corinthians teaches the Body’s standing and ministry in Christ:'), ('ul', ['comfort in affliction from the God of all comfort', 'a new creature in Christ (2 Cor. 5:17)', 'the ministry of reconciliation committed to Paul and his companions (2 Cor. 5:18–19)', 'the righteousness of God in Christ (2 Cor. 5:21)', 'grace sufficient in weakness (2 Cor. 12:9)']), ('p', 'It does not place the Body under Israel’s covenants and does not say more about the thorn in the flesh than Paul wrote.')]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What 2 Corinthians Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in 2 Corinthians and shows which ones belong to the Body of Christ. Each row lists a truth from the letter, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Paul’s audience and setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what 2 Corinthians teaches directly to the church today and keeps those truths separate from universal principles or reports that apply more broadly.'), ('p', 'Program Identity shows where a truth belongs. “Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth” marks a statement, command, warning or instruction given to the Body of Christ through Paul.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in 2 Corinthians', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Ministry of Reconciliation', 'God committed to Paul and his companions the word of reconciliation.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '2 Cor. 5:18–19'], ['A New Creature', 'Any man in Christ is a new creature; old things are passed away.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '2 Cor. 5:17'], ['The Righteousness of God', 'Christ was made sin for us that we might be made the righteousness of God in Him.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '2 Cor. 5:21'], ['Walking by Faith', 'We walk by faith, not by sight.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '2 Cor. 5:7'], ['The Judgment Seat of Christ', 'We must all appear before it, that each may receive for what he has done.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '2 Cor. 5:10'], ['Sufficient Grace', 'Christ’s grace is sufficient; His strength is made perfect in weakness.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '2 Cor. 12:9'], ['The New Testament Ministry', 'Paul’s ministry stays with the Mystery; the covenant itself belongs to Israel, and the Body benefits from Christ’s blood without being the covenant party.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '2 Cor. 3:6'], ['The Espousal', 'Paul has espoused them to one husband, to present as a chaste virgin to Christ.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '2 Cor. 11:2']], [22, 43, 17, 18])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in 2 Corinthians that apply to everyone, not just the Body of Christ. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Paul’s program-specific instructions, this section helps you see what 2 Corinthians teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches the church in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['The God of All Comfort', 'God comforts us in all our tribulation.', '2 Cor. 1:3–4'], ['A Cheerful Giver', 'God loveth a cheerful giver.', '2 Cor. 9:7'], ['God’s Power Made Perfect', 'His strength is made perfect in weakness.', '2 Cor. 12:9'], ['Sowing and Reaping', 'He which soweth sparingly shall reap also sparingly.', '2 Cor. 9:6'], ['Seen and Unseen', 'The things which are seen are temporal; the things which are not seen are eternal.', '2 Cor. 4:18'], ['Examine Yourselves', 'Examine yourselves, whether ye be in the faith.', '2 Cor. 13:5'], ['Walk by Faith', 'We walk by faith, not by sight.', '2 Cor. 5:7'], ['Be of One Mind', 'Be perfect, be of good comfort, be of one mind, live in peace.', '2 Cor. 13:11']], [30, 46, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of 2 Corinthians without mixing programs. The applications come directly from Paul’s instructions to the Body of Christ and from the universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the letter actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants or prophetic program — this grid helps you respond to 2 Corinthians in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Comfort one another</b> — Pass on the comfort received from God (2 Cor. 1:3–4).', '<b>Live as a new creature</b> — Walk in the newness Christ gives (2 Cor. 5:17).', '<b>Be reconciled and speak reconciliation</b> — Receive and share the word of reconciliation (2 Cor. 5:18–20).', '<b>Walk by faith</b> — Trust what is unseen (2 Cor. 5:7; 4:18).', '<b>Give willingly</b> — Give as purposed in the heart, not grudgingly (2 Cor. 9:7).', '<b>Rest in sufficient grace</b> — Boast in weaknesses (2 Cor. 12:9).', '<b>Examine yourselves</b> — Test whether you are in the faith (2 Cor. 13:5).', '<b>Live in peace</b> — Be of one mind and of good comfort (2 Cor. 13:11).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for 2 Corinthians', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach 2 Corinthians in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Comfort in Affliction (1:1–2:17) — The God of all comfort and Paul’s change of plans. <i>(2 Cor. 1:3–4)</i>', 'The Ministry and the Glory (Chs. 3–4) — The new testament ministry and treasure in earthen vessels. <i>(2 Cor. 3:6; 4:6–7)</i>', 'Reconciliation (Ch. 5) — A new creature, the judgment seat, and the word of reconciliation. <i>(2 Cor. 5:10, 17–21)</i>', 'Open Hearts (Chs. 6–7) — Paul’s appeal and the godly sorrow that works repentance. <i>(2 Cor. 6:11–13; 7:10)</i>', 'The Collection (Chs. 8–9) — Grace in giving and the cheerful giver. <i>(2 Cor. 8:9; 9:6–8)</i>', 'The Apostle’s Defense (Chs. 10–11) — His labours and his jealousy for the church. <i>(2 Cor. 11:2, 23–28)</i>', 'Weakness and Strength (Chs. 12–13) — The thorn, sufficient grace, and the call to self-examination. <i>(2 Cor. 12:7–10; 13:5)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read 2 Corinthians, helping you see both what the letter says to believers and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications draw only on what 2 Corinthians itself says, so your response stays tied to the text.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching 2 Corinthians simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group see what the letter states and what it leaves unsaid, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap framework, 2 Corinthians was written during the overlap and shows Paul’s ministry in the Mystery Program. This Companion shows you how to keep the new covenant’s own audience in its place while drawing out the principles that apply to all believers.')])]
